-- title:
--   Theorem 2, p.26 — the barrier policy is optimal for the EWF (10) with long-run average cost $\gamma$ when $b_j\mu_j/\lambda_j>c_k\mu_k$ (corrected: barrier property almost surely)
-- statement:
--   Suppose that $b_j\mu_j/\lambda_j>c_k\mu_k$ and fix $S\in\mathbb R^n_+$. Let $X$ be a Brownian motion with drift $0$ and variance $\Sigma$ on a filtered probability space with the usual conditions. The Bellman system (13) has a solution, and for each solution $(f,B,\gamma)$:
--
--   1. a barrier policy with upper barrier $B$ exists;
--   2. every process with the barrier property on every path is feasible for the EWF (10);
--   3. every barrier policy satisfies
--   $$\lim_{t\to\infty}\frac1t\,\mathbf E\Big[\sum_ib_i\int_0^t(a_i(s)-S_i)^+\,ds+c_k\mu_kE(t)\Big]=\gamma,$$
--   so its objective value is $\sum_ih_iS_i+\gamma$;
--   4. every feasible solution with this $S$ has objective value at least $\sum_ih_iS_i+\gamma$;
--   5. every feasible solution attaining this value has long-run average backorder and emergency repair cost $\gamma$.
--
--   This is the half of the paper's decision rule in which emergency repairs are worth using: the workload is reflected at $0$ and at $B$, and $B$ and $\gamma$ come from the Bellman system.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: barrier policy: C1–C3 for every path of X + θe (Definition 6, Lemma 4) → C1–C3 for almost every ω; (10b)–(10f) for every ω; reason: printed Lemma 4 is false: n=2, μ=(1,1), λ=(1,2), b=(1,3/2), c=(3/5,3/5), S=0, any h>0, θ≤0, Σ>0, so that bjμj/λj=3/4>3/5=ckμk. For (f,B,γ) of Lemma 2 Part ii, f′ rises strictly from 0 to 3/5 on [0,B], so f′(w₀)=1/2 at one w₀∈(0,B), and β(w)=w on [0,w₀), β(w)=2w on [w₀,B]. For x(t)=w₀/2+(3/2)w₀t no triple satisfies C1–C3 with b=B: w climbs to w₀, then the slope is at most −w₀/2 at or above w₀ and at least w₀/2 below it, so w can neither leave w₀ nor stay there
--   - reading: q* is "an optimal solution to (14)" (Definition 2), κ(w) chosen so that β is right continuous (OA p.26) → q* fixed: the fill of OA A.2 for w ≤ Σᵢ Sᵢ/μᵢ, otherwise κ with largest λ, then least index; reason: Definition 2 leaves ties open; this is the choice the paper names on OA pp.2 and 26
--   - reading: (10f): a(·,W(·)) is 𝔽-adapted → progressive measurability, and integrable paths (aᵢ − Sᵢ)⁺ on every [0,t]; reason: the paper integrates these paths in (10a)–(10b) and applies Tonelli in (A.47), which needs both
--   - encoding: a(t,W(t,ω),ω) → one process a t ω; reason: the allocation is used only along the path
--   - encoding: real-valued expectations in (10a) → ℝ≥0∞ with lower integrals; reason: the integrands are nonnegative and an infinite expectation stays infinite
--   - generalization: Σ := Σᵢ αᵢλᵢ(1+σᵢ²)/μᵢ² (p.15) → Σ > 0 a free parameter; reason: every statement depends on Σ only through Σ > 0; the source value is one such Σ
--   - generalization: 𝔽 generated by (Xᵢ), with the usual conditions → any filtration with the usual conditions for which X is a Brownian motion; reason: the augmented natural filtration is one such filtration
--   - encoding: SKU set {1,…,I}, indices j, k → Fin n with n ≥ 1; bjμj/λj and ckμk as minima; reason: only the values of the minima enter the statements
--   - encoding: f ∈ C₂ with domain ℝ₊ → f : ℝ → ℝ, C² on ℝ, with (13) imposed for w ≥ 0; reason: a C² function on [0,∞) extends to ℝ, so no solution is lost
--   - encoding: "is an optimal EWF (10) solution" → clauses 1–5: existence, feasibility, value, lower bound over every feasible solution, cost of every optimal solution; reason: this unfolds optimality and keeps the theorem non-vacuous
--   - encoding: C1's integral ∫₀ᵗ (drift)(w(y)) dy and C3: ∫₀^∞ w dℓ = ∫₀^∞ (b − w) du = 0 → the drift path y ↦ β(w(y)) interval-integrable on every [0,t]; dℓ, du as Stieltjes measures of ℓ, u extended by 0 to t < 0, giving no mass to {w > 0} resp. {w < b} on [0,∞); reason: β∘w is bounded and measurable for càdlàg w with values in [0,b], so integrability excludes no triple; since w ∈ [0,b] the two vanishing integrals say exactly that these sets are null
-- source:
--   Özkan and van Houtum, Joint Inventory and Scheduling Control in a Repair Facility, SSRN 3784144 (2023), Theorem 2, p.26

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_IsBellman
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_backorderRatio
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_IsBarrierPolicy
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_Model_objective
import Definitions.Def_RepairSched_UsualConditions
import Definitions.Def_AtaBarjesteh23_Workload_DriftBM

open MeasureTheory Filter Topology

namespace RepairSched

/-- **Theorem 2: the barrier policy is optimal for the EWF (10) when emergency repairs pay off.**
Özkan and van Houtum, *Joint Inventory and Scheduling Control in a Repair Facility*, SSRN 3784144 (2023), Theorem 2, p.26, with Definitions 5–6, p.25, and Lemma 2 Part ii, p.21.

Suppose that `bjμj/λj > ckμk` and fix `S ∈ ℝ^I₊`. On a filtered probability space with the usual
conditions carrying a Brownian motion `X` with drift `0` and variance `Σ`:
the Bellman system (13) has a solution, and for each solution `(f, B, γ)`,
(i) a barrier policy with upper barrier `B` exists;
(ii) every process with the barrier property on every path is feasible for the EWF (10);
(iii) every barrier policy has long-run average backorder and emergency repair cost `γ`, so its
objective is `Σᵢ hᵢSᵢ + γ`;
(iv) every feasible solution with this `S` has objective at least `Σᵢ hᵢSᵢ + γ`;
(v) every feasible solution that attains this value has long-run average backorder and emergency
repair cost `γ`.

**Formalization Note.** `(10)` fixes `S`, and costs are in `ℝ≥0∞`. Pathwise existence of the
regulator can fail for some paths (see `lemma_4`), so in (i) the barrier property holds for almost
every `ω` while (10b)–(10f) hold for every `ω`. -/
theorem theorem_2 {n : ℕ} [NeZero n] (M : Model n)
    (hEm : M.emergencyRate < M.backorderRatio) (S : Fin n → ℝ) (hS : ∀ i, 0 ≤ S i)
    {Ω : Type*} [mΩ : MeasurableSpace Ω] (𝔽 : Filtration ℝ mΩ) (P : Measure Ω)
    [IsProbabilityMeasure P] (h𝔽 : UsualConditions 𝔽 P) (X : ℝ → Ω → ℝ)
    (hX : AtaBarjesteh23.Workload.IsDriftBM 𝔽 P X 0 (Real.sqrt M.Sigma)) :
    (∃ f B γ, M.IsBellman S f B γ) ∧
    ∀ f B γ, M.IsBellman S f B γ →
      (∃ W I E a, M.IsBarrierPolicy 𝔽 P S f B X W I E a) ∧
      (∀ W I E, (∀ ω, M.IsBarrierPath S f B X W I E ω) →
        M.IsFeasible 𝔽 X S W I E (fun t ω => M.qStar S (deriv f (W t ω)) (W t ω))) ∧
      (∀ W I E a, M.IsBarrierPolicy 𝔽 P S f B X W I E a →
        Tendsto (M.avgBOE P S E a) atTop (𝓝 (ENNReal.ofReal γ)) ∧
        M.objective P S E a = ∑ i, ENNReal.ofReal (M.h i * S i) + ENNReal.ofReal γ) ∧
      (∀ W I E a, M.IsFeasible 𝔽 X S W I E a →
        ∑ i, ENNReal.ofReal (M.h i * S i) + ENNReal.ofReal γ ≤ M.objective P S E a) ∧
      ∀ W I E a, M.IsFeasible 𝔽 X S W I E a →
        M.objective P S E a = ∑ i, ENNReal.ofReal (M.h i * S i) + ENNReal.ofReal γ →
        limsup (M.avgBOE P S E a) atTop = ENNReal.ofReal γ := by sorry

end RepairSched
